# Security and privacy rules

- Apply least privilege in Django permissions, AWS IAM, database access, admin access, and presigned upload grants.
- Every object mutation performs an explicit object-level ownership or staff-role check.
- Use CSRF protection for browser mutations. Do not exempt endpoints unless the protocol requires it and compensating verification exists.
- Rate-limit and audit authentication, phone verification, listing submission, search abuse, report submission, and payment-session creation.
- Treat all user text, filenames, image metadata, query strings, webhook data, and third-party callbacks as untrusted.
- Direct uploads go to a private S3 staging prefix using short-lived, content-constrained grants. Validate magic bytes, actual dimensions, decoded size, count, ownership, and state before publishing.
- Re-encode accepted images, strip metadata including EXIF/GPS, generate controlled variants, and never serve staging objects publicly.
- Secrets come from environment/Secrets Manager. Never commit them, print them, place them in screenshots, or include them in agent prompts.
- Keep personal and sensitive data out of URLs, analytics labels, metrics dimensions, exception messages, and ordinary logs.
- Use generic public errors and detailed safe internal audit records.
- Security-sensitive changes require abuse cases and negative tests, not only happy paths.
- Record retention and deletion behavior must follow an accepted policy; do not invent one in code.
